KNOWNBUG
hierarchical_variable_index1.sv
--bound 2
^\[main\.p0\] always \(##1 main\.s\.mem\[\$past\(main\.i\)\] == 171\): PROVED up to bound 2$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
A procedural assignment whose lvalue is a hierarchical identifier selecting
an array element with a non-constant index (s.mem[i] <= ...) is rejected with
"unsupported lvalue for RTL construction: `hierarchical_identifier'" and a
CONVERSION ERROR (EXIT=2).

Writing to another module's variable through a hierarchical identifier is
permitted per IEEE 1800-2017, 23.6 and 23.7.  The same assignment with a
constant index (s.mem[2] <= ...) is accepted, and the equivalent non-
hierarchical write (regression/verilog/assignments/
assignment-to-non-constant-index1) is accepted too.

Root cause: in src/verilog/verilog_rtl.cpp, verilog_rtl_buildert::lower_lhs
has no case for ID_hierarchical_identifier, whereas decompose_lhs does (it
calls resolve_hierarchical_identifier).  When the index is constant,
decompose_lhs handles the lvalue; with a non-constant index decompose_lhs
returns no value and the code falls back to lower_lhs, which hits its final
else branch and throws "unsupported lvalue for RTL construction".
